  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual summary of how climate change and human activity affect extreme rainfall and flooding across a mountain-to-city-to-coast landscape. At the center, a storm cloud over mountains and an urban area links warmer, moister conditions to stronger precipitation. The main message is that more moisture strongly fuels heavier precipitation, while condensation releases more heat and can strengthen storms. Increased uplift also appears to raise the frozen fraction of precipitation and its intensity.\n\nThe diagram also shows several factors that can counteract or complicate this intensification. Cloud microphysics may limit rainfall growth, and a more stable atmosphere can inhibit storm formation. Pollution is shown as having mixed effects, suppressing rainfall in some cases but invigorating storms in others. A note at the top suggests that shifts in where the wettest events occur make local detection and projection harder.\n\nAt the surface, heavier rainfall increases flood severity, especially over cities where urbanization boosts storm impacts, runoff, and flash flooding. In river basins, runoff responses are complex and depend on catchment conditions and human intervention. More runoff can occur when intense rain falls on dry crusted or saturated soils, while increased water use can reduce river flow. Near the coast, intense rainfall combined with storm surge increases compound flooding.",
